      "tag": "Interactive Game Experience · Designing DataXCultures, KISD",
      "question": "How do smart technologies and AI companions influence our relationships with personal data, privacy and trust?",
      "text": "Imagine Cologne in 2076. A person is searching for their lost robotic dog, accompanied by an AI companion, and the city reveals itself through different layers of information. As a teaching assistant in KISD's Designing Data Cultures course, I contributed to research and discussions on smart cities, urban data infrastructures and how location-based technologies and AI companions shape the way we experience and navigate cities. This research inspired memo, a speculative educational game I co-developed with Jihyun and Texas State University. Two players wear modified red-and-blue 3D glasses, and each sees a different visual layer on the same screen; to follow the story and find the dog, they have to exchange information and combine their perspectives. Through this playful mechanic we explored data visibility, digital literacy, privacy and trust in future urban environments, and presented the prototype at an exhibition where visitors played it together.",
      "role": "Teaching assistance, research, game co-design",
